# Give an AI agent Trellis context

This guide explains how to give AI coding agents current Trellis instructions
when they work in a service or browser app repository.

The links below follow `main`. For a released installation, replace `main` with
its matching release tag so instructions and library APIs agree. The guides
cover Trellis-owned contracts and runtime behavior, not your application's
configuration parser, databases, architecture, or general development policies.

- Short guide:
  <https://raw.githubusercontent.com/qlever-llc/trellis/main/docs/static/llms.txt>
- Full guide:
  <https://raw.githubusercontent.com/qlever-llc/trellis/main/docs/static/llms-full.txt>
- TypeScript guide:
  <https://raw.githubusercontent.com/qlever-llc/trellis/main/docs/static/llms-typescript.txt>
- Svelte guide:
  <https://raw.githubusercontent.com/qlever-llc/trellis/main/docs/static/llms-svelte.txt>
- Rust guide:
  <https://raw.githubusercontent.com/qlever-llc/trellis/main/docs/static/llms-rust.txt>
- TypeScript AGENTS template: `/templates/trellis-service-typescript-AGENTS.md`
- Rust AGENTS template: `/templates/trellis-service-rust-AGENTS.md`
- Combined AGENTS template: `/templates/trellis-service-AGENTS.md`

## When to use each file

Use `llms.txt` as a small bootstrap document for every Trellis task. It points
an agent at the current service model, generated surfaces, and language-specific
guides.

Use `llms-full.txt` when the agent will change a contract, generated SDK usage,
service handlers, browser app wiring, operations, event publication, or
outbox/inbox code. It links to the language-specific TypeScript, Svelte, and
Rust guides.

For TypeScript SQL outbox work, `llms-typescript.txt` explains the
`service.createSqlOutbox(...)` executor and transaction boundary. Database
libraries, transaction ownership, and how handlers receive application
dependencies remain local choices.

Use `llms-typescript.txt` for TypeScript service work and `llms-rust.txt` for
Rust service work. Most service repositories should link one of these rather
than asking agents to read both language stacks.

Use `llms-svelte.txt` for Svelte or SvelteKit browser apps that need
`createTrellisApp`, `TrellisProvider`, native-IDL action selections, and import
resolution. `TrellisClientFor<typeof participant>` derives the connected client
type from its generated participant.

Adapt the TypeScript or Rust template into the repository's existing
`AGENTS.md`, then fill in the local commands and paths. Keep local rules in that
file; keep Trellis-wide rules linked to the raw guides. The combined template
remains available for mixed-language repositories.

## Recommended setup for a service repo

1. Download the matching template for review without overwriting existing
   instructions:

   ```sh
   curl -fsSL \
     https://raw.githubusercontent.com/qlever-llc/trellis/main/docs/static/templates/trellis-service-typescript-AGENTS.md \
      -o trellis-AGENTS.example.md
   ```

2. Fill in the repository-specific contract path, generation command, format
   command, typecheck command, test command, and run command. Include migration
   commands only if the application has them.
3. Link the raw `llms.txt`, `llms-full.txt`, the relevant language-specific
   guide URL, and the matching AGENTS template from any other AI-facing project
   instructions.
4. Ask agents to read the short guide for all Trellis tasks, the full guide for
   contract or runtime-surface changes, and the relevant language guide before
   editing service or browser app code.

## What to ask agents to verify

Ask agents to run the checks configured by the service repository, not an
invented standard task list. If IDL changed, ask them to run `trellis generate`
and verify generated artifacts are updated from the source contract.
